=== CELL A: ACROSS EVERY FACET, 'NOT REQUIRED' IS SILENCE ===

  An ad states the requirements it HAS. It does not enumerate the ones it LACKS.

  facet                            silent   share  explicitly absent   share
  visa_sponsorship                  10106  99.4%                 35   0.34%
  relocation_support                 9819  96.6%                  8   0.08%
  stated_working_language            9688  95.3%                 --      --
  application_language               9610  94.5%                 --      --
  norwegian_requirement_level        3707  36.5%                 93   0.91%

  There is no vector for a sentence that was never written. This is not a limitation
  of THIS encoder — it is a property of the data, and it holds for every facet.

=== CELL C: THE CEILING SPAN-LEVEL RETRIEVAL CANNOT PASS ===

  ads with a language evidence span at all: 6557 of 10166 (64.5%)
  of the 93 `explicitly_not_required`: 93 have a span (100%)
  of the 3707 `unstated`:              100 have a span (3%)

  An ad that never mentions language has no sentence to extract and no sentence to
  embed. Span-level indexing cannot reach it by construction — only a DECISION about
  how to treat silence can, which is what `retrieval/constraints.py` makes tunable.

=== CELL B: SPAN-LEVEL DISCRIMINATION ===

  93 accessible + 5703 demanding = 5796 ads; 5794 have a span.
  span embeddings from cache: (5796, 768)
  usable: 93 accessible spans vs 5701 demanding spans

  query                                     whole-ad  span-level   change
  NO positive — Jeg snakker flytende n         0.441       0.137   -0.304
  NO negated — Jeg snakker ikke norsk          0.393       0.170   -0.222
  EN positive — I speak fluent Norwegi         0.426       0.140   -0.285
  EN negated — I do not speak Norwegi          0.386       0.178   -0.208

  PAIRED POLARITY DELTA — negated minus positive, topic held fixed.
  A bi-encoder that handled negation would make this POSITIVE: saying 'not' should
  move you TOWARD the ads that do not require it.

           whole-ad   span-level
  NO         -0.048       +0.033
  EN         -0.040       +0.037

written reports/span_level_ceiling.json

=== VERDICT ===
  PARTLY SUSTAINED, AND THE SIGN IS NOT THE STORY.
    The polarity delta DOES flip positive on spans (+0.033 NO, +0.037 EN),
    so negation is WEAKLY representable once document noise is removed. Document noise
    was a real confound and the objection was correct to raise it.
    BUT absolute AUC COLLAPSES from 0.441 whole-ad to 0.178 at span level.
    An index like this ranks the accessible ads in the bottom fifth (0.14-0.18).
    Removing the job content removed the ONLY material the query and the ad shared:
    the accessible ad's span talks about ENGLISH, the query talks about NORWEGIAN.
    Span-level indexing sharpens the vocabulary mismatch it was meant to fix.
    The delta is also below the 0.05 materiality floor, so it is a direction, not
    a usable signal.

  SO THE ARCHITECTURE CONCLUSION STANDS, FOR A CORRECTED REASON. Negation is not
  literally unrepresentable — a small correctly-signed signal exists. It is far too
  weak to overcome the vocabulary gap between a seeker negating Norwegian and an ad
  affirming English. A typed predicate does not have to overcome anything.

  AND THE CEILING IS UNMOVED EITHER WAY: 3707 ads (36.5%) say nothing about
  language at all, and only 100 of them have any span to embed. No encoder reaches
  that population at any granularity, because there is no text. It needs a policy —
  which is what `constraints.py` makes tunable, and why severity for silence is 0.5
  rather than 1.0.
